{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_gain",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The target slope is proportional to the instantaneous temperature difference with one constant gain thermal_conductance/heat_capacity_A, so for fixed heat capacities all trajectories differ only by a rescaled contrast.",
      "child_relation": "Retain heat flow driven by T_B-T_A but let the mutual conductance depend on temperature level, e.g. stronger coupling at higher absolute temperatures, so the target depends on both contrast and mean temperature.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "T_A",
        "T_B",
        "thermal_conductance"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "For runs with the same temperature difference but different mean temperature, the parent predicts the same dT_A_dt while the child predicts faster equilibration at higher mean temperature; this appears as different early-time slope and non-exponential contrast decay curvature across such conditions."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "delta_T",
        "expression": "T_B - T_A",
        "role": "driving temperature contrast for inter-body heat flow"
      },
      {
        "name": "mean_T",
        "expression": "(T_A + T_B)/2",
        "role": "overall temperature level that can modulate contact conductance"
      },
      {
        "name": "pair_energy_proxy",
        "expression": "heat_capacity_A*T_A + heat_capacity_B*T_B",
        "role": "conserved total thermal energy proxy for the isolated pair"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when admissible experiments span a temperature range large enough that contact properties vary with absolute temperature while both bodies remain well mixed and positive in temperature; especially compare cases with similar |T_B-T_A| but different mean_T.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training, varying initial temperatures changes both delta_T and mean_T; in holdout, the same temperature-dependent conductance extends to unseen temperature levels, revealing curvature beyond a single shared exponential rate.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit of temperature-independent conductance, i.e. vanishing conductance temperature sensitivity over the explored range.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The model assumes the thermal contact conductance is constant, omitting realistic temperature dependence of interfacial or radiative-assisted heat transfer strength.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The thermal conductance between the bodies is constant and has no time delay.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Temperature-dependent mutual conductance of the thermal link, while preserving antisymmetric energy exchange between the two isolated bodies.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "T_A",
      "T_B",
      "thermal_conductance"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the constant-conductance assumption rather than add a new reservoir or state, because the same exchange process is already present but idealized. Hold fixed heat_capacity_A and heat_capacity_B and compare trajectories generated from different initial temperature levels. In the parent, dT_A_dt depends only on delta_T through one shared gain, so two conditions with equal delta_T but different mean_T must have identical target values and the same exponential relaxation rate after parameter refit. In the child, conductance varies with temperature level, so the mapping from observed states to dT_A_dt changes directly: higher mean_T can produce a larger magnitude slope even at the same delta_T, and the contrast decay is no longer exactly single-rate exponential. This is observable under the actual protocol because T_A and T_B are supplied evolving states. The refinement remains scientifically admissible if conductance stays positive, ensuring heat still flows to reduce |T_B-T_A|, pair energy remains conserved by equal-and-opposite exchange terms, and positive temperatures are maintained. Limitations: if all experiments stay in a very narrow temperature band, the effect may be too small and the parent may remain adequate."
  },
